<<<
content(ment). The two forms now mean practically the same, contentment having almost lost its verbal use (The contentment of his wishes left him unhappy) & meaning, like content, contented state.
===
**content(ment).** The two forms now mean practically the same, *contentment* having almost lost its verbal use (*The contentment of his wishes left him unhappy*) & meaning, like *content,* contented state.
>>>

<<<
Contentment is the usual word, content surviving chiefly in to heart’s content & as a poetic or rhetorical variant.
===
*Contentment* is the usual word, *content* surviving chiefly in *to heart's content* & as a poetic or rhetorical variant.
>>>

<<<
content(s), what is contained. The OED says ‘The stress conte’nt is historical, & still common among the educated °.
